I fixed it: /permissions now lists the deny entries that /cd added, so after /cd into a project with [directories] deny it no longer says "none (full filesystem access)" while the file tools are refusing those directories. Committed as bb046661. Format, clippy and the full cargo test suite all pass.

The problem. handle_permissions only read the restrictions set at launch. With nothing set at startup and a /cd directory deny of /proj/secret, it still printed Directory restrictions: none (full filesystem access). The bash section above it had the same problem: a deny pattern added by /cd still showed Command patterns: none configured. That's the same false claim in the same function, so I fixed both, even though the task only named directories.

The fix.
• The text is now built by a new pure function, permissions_report(...), in a new file, src/commands_permissions.rs. It's a new file because src/commands_config.rs was already 12 lines over the 2000-line module cap; it's now 1967 lines.
• `handle_permissions reads the two /cd` lists and prints the report.
• The "none" lines appear only when both the startup list and the /cd list are empty.
• Entries added by `/cd print in the same red ✗` style as startup entries, not dimmed, because they are live policy.
• Each /cd entry is labelled (a [directories] deny entry applied by /cd) (the existing fence wording) or (a deny entry applied by /cd).
• `/cd entries come from the project's .yoyo.toml`, so they're cleaned of control characters before display. Startup entries print exactly as before.

Tests. There are 7 new tests:
• With no `/cd entries (both lists empty, or startup only, with auto-approve on and off), the output matches the old text exactly (assert_eq! against a line-by-line copy of the old println!` calls).
• With /cd entries, the output equals the old text with only the changed section replaced.
• With both startup and /cd entries, each is shown with its own label.
• A hostile entry containing an escape byte doesn't reach the output raw.

To prove the main tests can fail, I temporarily made the function ignore the /cd directory list and restored it in the same command. The two directory tests failed by name, the unchanged-output tests stayed green, and it was green again after restoring, with no sabotage marker left. I didn't run /permissions in a live session; the "before" text comes from that temporarily broken run and the copy of the old code.

Docs. One sentence in docs/src/configuration/permissions.md and a new #869 entry in ARCHITECTURE.md. The entry notes that I found this by grepping restrictions.is_empty(). The existing guard (UNWIRED_CHECK_PATH_READERS) only counts code that checks paths, so any surface that just displays the policy is invisible to it. I didn't search for other such displays.

Still open on #869: commands_project.rs, context.rs and skill_fence.rs still ignore the /cd list. Those are the three files the guard still lists as unwired. The [permissions] allow / [directories] allow, hooks and MCP halves are also still open. The commit says "Part of #869", so the issue stays open.
